// BT-3377: An Actor with an ordinary method literally named `value` (a plain
// Integer accessor) had every EXTERNAL call to it wrapped as `{#ok, Value}`
// instead of returning `Value` directly. Root cause: the compiler's
// block-invocation intrinsics (`value`/`value:`/`value:value:`/
// `value:value:value:`/`valueWithArguments:` — see `intrinsics.rs`) generate
// a runtime `erlang:is_function/2` guard for any receiver whose type isn't
// known at compile time, since Blocks compile to plain Erlang funs and a
// variable holding an actor is statically indistinguishable from one holding
// a block. The guard's "not a function" fallback dispatched via the
// low-level `beamtalk_primitive:send/3`, which — unlike the proper generic
// dispatcher `beamtalk_message_dispatch:send/3` — does not know how to
// unwrap an actor's `{ok, Result} | {error, Error}` gen_server reply
// envelope (BT-918); it forwards `gen_server:call/2`'s raw reply as-is. The
// internal `self.value` read (a field access, not a message send) was never
// affected — only the selector-`value`-shaped external message send was.
//
// The bug was never about the literal string "value" — it reproduces for
// every selector in Block's `value`-family protocol, which is why this
// fixture also exercises `value:`/`value:value:`/`value:value:value:`/
// `valueWithArguments:` methods, not just the unary case from the original
// repro.

Actor subclass: BT3377ValueProbe
  state: value :: Integer = 0

  setIt -> BT3377ValueProbe =>
    self.value := 99
    self

  // Deliberately named after Block's own protocol — see fixture doc above.
  value -> Integer => self.value

  value: a -> Integer => self.value + a

  value: a value: b -> Integer => self.value + a + b

  value: a value: b value: c -> Integer => self.value + a + b + c

  valueWithArguments: args -> Integer =>
    self.value + (args inject: 0 into: [:acc :each | acc + each])
